When generating a Git commit message, follow Conventional Commits. Output only the message.

Format:
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

Types: feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, perf, test, build, ci, chore, revert.
Scope is optional. Use a short area when one is obvious (web, domain, server). Omit it when the change spans the repo.

Subject rules:
- Imperative present tense: "add", "fix", "remove". Never "added" or "adds".
- Lowercase. No trailing period.
- One line, about 50 characters, never over 72.
- Say what changed and why it matters. Do not list files or restate the diff.

Body:
- Default to the subject line only.
- Add a body only when the reason is not obvious from the subject.
- Separate it with one blank line. Wrap near 72 characters.
- Explain why, not a line-by-line changelog.

Breaking changes: put ! after the type or scope, and add a footer line `BREAKING CHANGE: <what broke>`.
If the diff clearly closes an issue, add a footer `Fixes #123`. Never invent an issue number.

Good:
feat(web): add household invitation flow
fix(domain): keep split totals exact in cents
docs: describe package dependency boundaries

Bad:
Added a new feature for invitations.
Update files
feat: Added household invitations.
